<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Puducherry (union territory)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pondicherry (union territory)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Puducherry is a union territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> formed from the four former French settlements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Pondicherry</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaikal">Karaikal</a>, Mahé and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yanam">Yanam</a>, which lie hundreds of kilometres apart. They were transferred to India de facto in 1954 and legally in 1962, and became a union territory in 1963. Its capital is the city of Puducherry, and French retains official status alongside Tamil, Malayalam, Telugu and English.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/puducherry-union-territory.jpg" alt="Puducherry (union territory)" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karthik Easvur · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pondicherry-Rock_beach_aerial_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Union Territory of Puducherry lies on the southern part of the Indian Peninsula and consists of four small districts that do not touch one another. Puducherry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaikal">Karaikal</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>, are enclaves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yanam">Yanam</a>, also on the Bay of Bengal, is an enclave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>; and Mahé, on the Laccadive Sea, is an enclave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>. Together they cover 483 square kilometres. Some regions are themselves made of separate pockets: Puducherry has eleven and Mahé three. This unusual geography is a legacy of the colonial period, since the territory keeps the borders of former French India. Its name comes from the Tamil putu-cheri, meaning new village, first spelt Poudichéry by the French.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest recorded history of the area goes back to the second century CE, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea">Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</a> mentions a marketplace called Poduke. Excavations at Arikamedu, a site possibly connected with it, found Roman pottery; later investigation placed the occupation of the site from the third or second century BCE to the eighth century CE.</p><p>In 1674 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Pondicherry</a> became a French colony. With Chandernagor, French since 1673, Mahé (since 1721), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yanam">Yanam</a> (since 1723), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaikal">Karaikal</a> (since 1739) and Masulipatam (1760) it formed French India under a single governor in Pondicherry, although British occupations repeatedly interrupted French rule over one or more of these enclaves. The territories passed to the Republic of India de facto on 1 November 1954 and de jure on 16 August 1962. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandannagar">Chandannagar</a> merged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a> in 1954. The remaining four became the State of Pondicherry, and on 1 July 1963 the Union Territory of Pondicherry; the name was officially changed to Puducherry on 1 October 2006.</p><p>Under the Treaty of Cession of 1956 the four territories kept the special administrative status they had before 1 November 1954, and any constitutional change to it was to follow consultation of the population. The treaty also kept French as an official language for as long as the elected representatives did not decide otherwise.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Puducherry is governed as a union territory, with a Lieutenant Governor representing the central government and an elected legislative assembly. At the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> its population was 1,394,467. Tamil is the first language of most people, with Telugu and Malayalam spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yanam">Yanam</a> and Mahé; the official languages are French, Tamil, Telugu, Malayalam and English. The city of Puducherry was the residence of Sri Aurobindo, and the Sri Aurobindo Ashram and the experimental city of Auroville lie in and beside it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Puducherry (union territory)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
